#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092017 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4daa6bc-1c9a-5c23-8032-4d70435d9388" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "90308076-a0ef-5e6a-900d-2ef4c8107907" :question/math-academy-id "e-5466" :question/problem "The vector $\\mathbf{v}$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ 10 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $\\mathbf{v}?$" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by $T^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-15-(-20)}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 2 \\\\ -10 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092018 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/id #uuid "7969785b-fc71-50f4-9d73-da5599630b6e" :question/math-academy-id "e-5467" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $\\mathbf T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle $S'$ under the action of $\\mathbf T.$ The triangle $S'$ has vertices $(4,3),(4,10),$ and $(-4,-3).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(1,0)$\n\n- $(4,-3)$\n\n- $(-1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of $S'\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by $T^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{4-(-3)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 & -4 \\\\ 3 & 10 & -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{7}[\\begin{aligned}7 & 14 & -7 \\\\ 0 & 28 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(1,0),(2,4),(-1,0).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302679 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4daa6bc-1c9a-5c23-8032-4d70435d9388" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2bcd7fe9-bbce-56c8-8b94-23719607f35f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{5}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302681} {:answer/id #uuid "12cf0e3d-820a-5c9c-b73a-6e0ec70ba8d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302682} {:answer/id #uuid "5754939e-dbec-587b-8981-d4821d47ca8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302683} {:answer/id #uuid "ed9e9a04-9e65-51ee-812f-cfb87c57edfa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302684} {:answer/id #uuid "ae7d025c-fe6d-519b-806f-355da3ca5d56" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302685}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5754939e-dbec-587b-8981-d4821d47ca8b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{10}\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302683} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c2e3fa2-9857-5f2e-92c6-3a8c28e14e44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302680}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c41ad31a-6e3e-586e-8ef8-be80c0201af2" :question/math-academy-id "q-11972" :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ -2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2-(-8)}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{5} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{10}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302686 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0de5b9f-d890-5e1b-b316-fdf28ff903ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302688} {:answer/id #uuid "9716162c-5b0f-57e1-88ea-bf0cb95f2b77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302689} {:answer/id #uuid "33d9b464-0f9e-50ae-a095-50d2b6538733" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302690} {:answer/id #uuid "2c1c5245-8b77-5f5d-9eea-2cdb2accd99a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302691} {:answer/id #uuid "00c44b4c-e34f-50bc-9625-41c29776eb9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0de5b9f-d890-5e1b-b316-fdf28ff903ef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302688} :answer-field/id #uuid "1316c83c-b72a-50b4-bdbc-2de0845dfa18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302687}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "98953b86-1970-5f9b-baa4-31123d8311f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-12273" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe quadrilateral $Q$ is mapped to the quadrilateral ${Q}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The quadrilateral ${Q}^{′}$ has vertices $(2,-5),(3,-6),(4,-8),$ and $(3,-7).$ Which of the following are vertices of $Q?$\n\n- $(-5,-2)$\n- $(-6,-3)$\n- $(-8,-4)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${Q}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $Q$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0-(-1)}[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & -6 & -8 & -7 \\\\ -2 & -3 & -4 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $Q$ are $(-5,-2),(-6,-3),(-8,-4),(-7,-3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302693 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4daa6bc-1c9a-5c23-8032-4d70435d9388" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb42fce5-ecd9-5be3-8a06-9ce1701778f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302695} {:answer/id #uuid "832a3e97-6273-5c6c-bf32-74a9d7a73db4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb42fce5-ecd9-5be3-8a06-9ce1701778f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302695} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbd82ae8-be8e-565e-9461-888d979ed6b4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302694} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cc87bf11-e811-57a8-b65c-4b8e7dc53b71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cc87bf11-e811-57a8-b65c-4b8e7dc53b71" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302698} :answer-field/id #uuid "1996a243-2e6d-59d5-9878-d8191a39239b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302697}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "01b27500-efae-5a5d-b765-ef5c3bf7a8d6" :question/math-academy-id "q-276283" :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -8 \\\\ -2 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{20-16}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 8 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302699 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4daa6bc-1c9a-5c23-8032-4d70435d9388" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6305ec90-193f-577d-875a-7066776262e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302701} {:answer/id #uuid "baa34db1-5970-5aa4-b084-0352a869408e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "baa34db1-5970-5aa4-b084-0352a869408e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302702} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cfab445-ffaf-5c68-8be7-c89c236ff4ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302700} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aed8cee-ed81-5be7-9b26-c8fe2d2b0134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2aed8cee-ed81-5be7-9b26-c8fe2d2b0134" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{22}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302704} :answer-field/id #uuid "79655dbc-b505-53f2-ad4b-8f78e546db9d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302703}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4bf62a04-752f-5b6c-afdb-534f8df78167" :question/math-academy-id "q-317429" :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 5 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$\n\n| $v=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-2}} |  |  |" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{12+10}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{22}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 19\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{11} \\\\ \\frac{19}{22}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292352 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4daa6bc-1c9a-5c23-8032-4d70435d9388" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ba98cd4-d69c-5495-af9b-72830b9e89cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292354} {:answer/id #uuid "36bb4c72-2fc5-5d15-a061-2e738b5c790e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292355} {:answer/id #uuid "91da35ad-83ab-5130-a473-bb967c133713" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292356} {:answer/id #uuid "b18890cf-1605-52fe-8586-d087db666e09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292357} {:answer/id #uuid "72971519-5585-5650-8c38-d018bd7e9b86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ba98cd4-d69c-5495-af9b-72830b9e89cf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292354} :answer-field/id #uuid "04039f26-3365-5f29-bdb0-7f8b8b5a33c5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292353}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c1e4171a-6ffc-51f5-860a-01422535957d" :question/math-academy-id "q-85077" :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{6-5}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -5 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}13 \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302705 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d4daa6bc-1c9a-5c23-8032-4d70435d9388" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Vector Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c7728da-afa3-5b21-9819-8db41f35140c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302707} {:answer/id #uuid "f59723f7-1fcc-5039-a218-d862299bcc2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302708} {:answer/id #uuid "4d94ba2b-162f-591c-9876-bfc654ec85b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302709} {:answer/id #uuid "63031ba3-d181-50b5-81f9-e5df67880862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302710} {:answer/id #uuid "31828af4-eea0-56e5-86ae-13e915ddfb13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302711}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "63031ba3-d181-50b5-81f9-e5df67880862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302710} :answer-field/id #uuid "c8f0fcd7-64a0-50ca-9feb-2549a6928d78" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302706}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0495596a-94f8-5b12-b35f-68e0ed70a3ff" :question/math-academy-id "q-85171" :question/problem "The vector $v$ in the plane is mapped to the vector $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ under the action of the linear transformation $T$ whose matrix $T$ is given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWhat is the original vector $v?$" :question/worked-solution "From the given information, we have \n\n$$\nT\\cdot v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to calculate the inverse of $T$, which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{18-16}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original vector is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 6\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186283871 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91f5c48f-d05f-5cff-90ae-d12bfc8eb888" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283873} {:answer/id #uuid "d909f321-0332-526d-b496-6ace0c6f7579" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283874} {:answer/id #uuid "28e3ef56-12f4-504b-801b-f27f1626be7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283875} {:answer/id #uuid "e46f312a-9b66-5ace-a5f8-651db7728461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283876} {:answer/id #uuid "54c30f94-9552-5c2a-80c6-1245bbc63410" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e46f312a-9b66-5ace-a5f8-651db7728461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283876} :answer-field/id #uuid "765daac3-6ce4-521c-ba95-5db5eea21b30" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283872}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "187a3494-862e-562d-b358-18813743f678" :question/math-academy-id "q-9255" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(3,2),(14,6),$ and $(9,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(3,2)$\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(5,1)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{2-0}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & -4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 & 14 & 9 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 & 10 \\\\ 2 & 6 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(-1,1),(2,3),(5,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac9ab4f6-655f-5818-9d98-0406f23b03ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302714} {:answer/id #uuid "d8d70f60-3b31-5a23-a530-e4b4fbf03eff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302715} {:answer/id #uuid "2eec686d-7f4a-52ad-bf7f-ecce79e7a494" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302716} {:answer/id #uuid "158f054f-766a-5ffe-b731-3f1d88b0d02d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302717} {:answer/id #uuid "b05b4f64-0a6b-5987-aff1-8038340059ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac9ab4f6-655f-5818-9d98-0406f23b03ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302714} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5ea3ee6-c419-50f1-b790-79f416f0de76" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302713}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6142d263-231c-53b8-832c-47907c585c3a" :question/math-academy-id "q-9258" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe triangle $S$ is mapped to the triangle ${S}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The triangle ${S}^{′}$ has vertices $(4,-1),(17,-3),$ and $(10,-1).$ Which of the following are vertices of $S?$\n\n- $(3,2)$\n- $(-1,1)$\n- $(5,1)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${S}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $S$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{-1-0}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 17 & 10 \\\\ -1 & -3 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of the vertices of the original triangle are $(-1,1),(2,3),(5,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f763be17-07f9-5202-929d-0569f45dad17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302721} {:answer/id #uuid "c0b43e18-f376-51a5-b559-7275d6dd9404" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302722} {:answer/id #uuid "a4690e49-98cb-500a-8a24-2a5450a75394" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302723} {:answer/id #uuid "776c663b-0fbb-54b2-9cf3-976f43c93aef" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302724} {:answer/id #uuid "1498a4c9-87e4-5710-a9a2-9ef8d1924f63" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302725}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f763be17-07f9-5202-929d-0569f45dad17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302721} :answer-field/id #uuid "5584d02d-df96-50c4-89a3-b26deebd1b8e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302720}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "965eaccb-b8c7-5f4b-a1ea-f7ee3a9791ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-9262" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(2,-5),(3,-6),(4,-8),$ and $(3,-7).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n\n- $(1,3)$\n- $(3,3)$\n- $(2,3)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{0-1}[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 4 & 3 \\\\ -5 & -6 & -8 & -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 & 4 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(1,2),(3,3),(4,4),(2,3).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0cbb889-0fe1-5347-94a3-53db3ff9a95c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertices of a Polygon Given Its Image Under a Linear Transformation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66a7f402-aecc-5624-9d20-8866c52765a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292368} {:answer/id #uuid "e49af0fc-933e-5e31-ace0-05d5066b6079" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292369} {:answer/id #uuid "12cf6aa2-2da2-5697-b58b-18c03200ffd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292370} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8971b1-0e3a-5c9f-b154-e0e170c86f30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292371} {:answer/id #uuid "4aa3c7ed-ee51-52e5-8e20-281743c9ac15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292372}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4aa3c7ed-ee51-52e5-8e20-281743c9ac15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292372} :answer-field/id #uuid "90d05ff4-b74f-5e7e-a6fe-cb615b2a22f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186292367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d31fcdd1-6a95-5433-a4d6-af70cdf371e8" :question/math-academy-id "q-9263" :question/problem "Consider the linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe parallelogram $P$ is mapped to the parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ under the action of $T.$ The parallelogram ${P}^{′}$ has vertices $(1,1),(0,0),(-1,1),$ and $(0,2).$ Which of the following are vertices of $P?$\n\n- $(0,-1)$\n- $(1,1)$\n- $(-1,0)$" :question/worked-solution "First, we create a matrix $Y$ containing all of the vertices of ${P}^{′}:$ \n\n$$\nY=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, if $X$ denotes the matrix that contains the corresponding vertices of $P$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T\\cdot X & =Y \\\\ T\\cdot X & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe need to reverse the action of the matrix $T.$ So, we multiply the above equation by ${T}^{-1},$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1}\\cdot T\\cdot X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\ X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe inverse of $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{1-(-1)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & ={T}^{-1}\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, the coordinates of $P$ are $(1,0),(0,0),(0,1),(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}]}
